<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Mersal.Ingredients</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Mersal.Ingredients</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    No ProjectReference, and nothing that touches I/O. This is the platform's ONE answer to "what molecule
    is this, and what molecules is this product made of" — a question asked by the prescribing engine (to
    look a manufacturer label up), by masterdata (to decompose a product into ingredients so an allergy,
    an interaction or a duplicate-therapy rule can be keyed on one), and by the loader.

    It lives in its own library because the alternative is worse in both directions. Leaving it inside
    Mersal.ClinicalValidation would make masterdata — reference data, which sits BELOW the engine — depend
    on the engine. Copying it into masterdata would give the platform two normalisers, and two
    implementations of a matching rule diverge silently: the day they disagree, a drug resolves to one
    ingredient when a rule is authored and a different one when a prescription is screened against it.
  -->
</Project>
